def change(prefix,new):
 hits=[p for p in ps if p['text'].startswith(prefix)]
 assert len(hits)==1,(prefix,len(hits))
 p=hits[0];patches.append({'start':p['start'],'old':p['text'],'new':new.rstrip()+'\n'})
change('The published source reference is', '''The published source reference is tducn110/basicproject at revision 7e1e2d51321a4e6b2e26493c34f0a485d4de39cc. CaroGame contains the application and its WebAssembly package; present contains the teaching website. The weekly folders, benchmark and research contain documentation and experimental records. The Rust baseline in research/current_2026_10_23/baseline_engine is an earlier implementation; the modified engine is identified by hashes and selected excerpts [1], [9], [10], [11]. A local follow-up on 13 November 2026 includes that modified source in CaroGame/engine and rebuilds the application package from it. Appendix B records this release separately from the pinned experiments, and Appendix C gives both reproduction paths.''')
change('Reproducibility and provenance. The repository supplies', '''Reproducibility and provenance. The repository supplies raw measurements, harnesses, source excerpts and binary hashes. The 12 November follow-up matched 36 available engine, reference, harness and lock files to their archived hashes and reproduced the non-timing search results. On 13 November, the modified engine was included in the local application source package and rebuilt to the same WebAssembly bytes. The native reference library remains a separately identified dependency. Appendix B records these checks without replacing the dates or environments of the historical experiments.''')
change('The measurements cover a finite corpus', '''The measurements cover a finite corpus and specific native or browser environments. The published Git revision includes the application and binary but omits the complete modified engine and caro-core-reference dependency. The local release now supplies the modified engine and a reproducible application build; reproducing the separate reference experiments still requires their dependency sources. Historical timing and bot-match summaries retain narrower provenance than the controlled research records. Physical-device interaction, live host integration, human usability and calibrated playing strength remain subjects for further evaluation [1], [11], [13], [24], [46], [52].''')
change('Reproducibility is the first development priority.', '''Release maintenance should preserve the source-to-binary connection established by the local rebuild. The modified engine, lockfile, build command and generated-package hashes should be published together. The separate reference library and experiment harnesses should also be distributed with their exact dependencies. Each new experiment should identify its source revision, toolchain, platform and binary hash, while the earlier baseline remains available under its own identity.''')
change('The recorded native engine experiment reports 408', '''The recorded native engine experiment reports 408 Minimax/Alpha-Beta comparisons with matching values and 820 successful restoration checks. Separate adapter and browser records exercise the connection between search and the application. In the reference experiments, move ordering reduced visits on some positions, but its evaluation work could outweigh the time saved. A hard cap discarded immediate wins in one fixture, and cache reuse after a root-player change returned an incorrect score in another. These results show that depth and node count alone do not assess correctness or efficiency [19], [22], [27], [31], [32], [49].
The final local application provides all six report profiles: Easy, Normal, Hard, Very Hard, Expert and Master. A fresh build from the included Rust source reproduced the existing application binary byte-for-byte. Browser smoke checks selected each profile and observed a response with completed depths 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 on the checked opening. The presentation now embeds this local application. These checks establish the tested configuration and interaction; playing-strength calibration remains future work.''')
change('The following procedure inspects the published revision', '''The first procedure inspects the published revision and runs its frontend scripts. The local release described later in this appendix additionally contains CaroGame/engine and the rebuild script. Its changes are supplied in the local source package; checking out the published SHA alone does not retrieve them. Native research records retain their original harnesses, manifests, results and provenance [1], [11], [13].''')
change('The original reference runner requires caro-core-reference', '''The original reference runner requires caro-core-reference at the repository root. The current-engine research runner expects gomoku-main/engine and an application directory named 10_caro; its WASM stage copies generated files into that application. These paths belong to the original experiment workspace. The local application release instead owns its Rust source at CaroGame/engine and uses the following commands from CaroGame to rebuild it. Rust with the wasm32-unknown-unknown target and wasm-pack must be installed; the offline build also requires the locked Cargo dependencies in the local cache.
npm run build:wasm
cargo test --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml --locked --offline
npm run typecheck
npm test
npm run build
The build writes src/game/wasm/engine/provenance.json with the input hashes, output hashes and tool versions. The 13 November build used rustc/cargo 1.98.1 and wasm-pack 0.15.0. The current engine toolchain selects stable, so a later build must record the resolved version and compare its generated hashes rather than assume identical bytes.
For the local defense demonstration, start the game with npm run dev from CaroGame, then start npm run dev from present in another terminal. The game uses port 8443 and the presentation uses port 5174. Local presentation entry points use the game on the same host at port 8443; VITE_CARO_GAME_URL can explicitly select another application endpoint. Open http://localhost:5174 for the presentation or http://localhost:8443 for the game.
The local release contains all six difficulty selectors and uses the rebuilt Worker/WASM route. The external 10-caro.vercel.app deployment was not updated in this local release and should not be used to demonstrate its six-tier contract. Regenerating the separate native reference datasets still requires their exact dependency sources and original harness layout [11], [13].''')
